# Your copy of Kav's server. The setup page (install.sh) writes .env for you; this file is what each line means, for
# setting it by hand instead: copy it to .env, fill it in, and run: docker compose up -d
# Each time the setup page saves, it rewrites the lines it manages; KAV_APP_KEY, LISTEN, PORT, REBUILD_AT, SETUP_LISTEN,
# SETUP_PORT, MOT_URL and MOT_URL_BACKUP are kept as you set them.

# The parts that run: planner (Kav's planner), predictor (Kav's arrival times), https (a certificate for DOMAIN),
# official (a second planner on the ministry's own times, with OFFICIAL_URL below), translate (the alerts in English,
# with TRANSLATE_URL below). Comma-separated.
COMPOSE_PROFILES=planner,predictor

# Live buses from the Ministry of Transport's real-time service (SIRI). It answers only the servers it has approved,
# each with a key of its own, for one IP address: apply on its page,
# https://www.gov.il/he/departments/general/real_time_information_siri
# Without one (and without LIVE_URL), no live buses here: this server does the rest.
MOT_KEY=

# Live buses from another source instead, anything answering SIRI StopMonitoring as the ministry does (server/LIVE.md):
# a GTFS-Realtime feed through server/examples/gtfsrt_siri.py, say. Its key, if any, goes in MOT_KEY.
#LIVE_URL=http://my-source:8100/siri

# One area instead of all of Israel, south,west,north,east: the live buses keep to it, and Kav's planner is built for it
# alone (server/selfhost/README.md, "One area, for a smaller machine"). With it, set BUILD_MEMORY=4 and PLANNER_MEMORY=2
# below, as the setup page does.
#BOUNDS=31.95,34.70,32.20,34.95

# Who may use it: accounts (each person signs each phone in, made on the setup page's People), open (anyone, 120
# requests a minute each), or key (Kav's own server: phones sign with the key official builds of the app carry).
ACCESS=accounts

# The server's own key: its services sign with it (and phones, with ACCESS=key). Any text of 16 characters or more;
# the setup page makes one. `openssl rand -base64 18` makes one too.
KAV_APP_KEY=

# HTTPS, which Kav needs: with a domain name pointing at this machine and ports 80 and 443 open, the https profile above
# and DOMAIN get a certificate from Let's Encrypt and keep it renewed.
#DOMAIN=kav.example.com

# With a proxy or tunnel of your own instead (Cloudflare Tunnel, Nginx Proxy Manager): send all of https://<address>/ to
# port 8080 on this machine, and give that address here. Trip links are made on it.
#PUBLIC_URL=https://kav.example.com

# Trip planning. on (the default): Kav's planner, with the planner profile above. off: no planner, no routes from this
# server, no nightly planner build. own: a planner of your own (server/PLANNER.md), at one of the two addresses below.
#PLANNER=off
#PLANNER=own
#OTP_URL=http://my-otp:8080/otp/gtfs/v1
#PLAN_URL=http://my-planner:9000/plan

# A second planner, on the ministry's own times, whose routes come after Kav's own: as much memory again while it
# answers, and a second build each night. On with the official profile above and this line.
#OFFICIAL_URL=http://otp-official:8080/otp/gtfs/v1

# Arrival times (server/PREDICTOR.md). Kav's own run with the predictor profile above. A model of your own for it: its
# model pack's address and the pack's sha256 (the setup page fills both in when you give it the address).
#MODELS_URL=https://github.com/me/kav/releases/download/v1/model.tar.gz
#MODELS_SHA256=
# A predictor of your own running anywhere, answering the predictions file at an address (asked every 15 seconds).
#PREDICT_URL=http://my-predictor:8000/now.json
# Where the predicted times are read from on this machine: Kav's predictor writes them there, and so can yours.
# Empty: none, the ministry's own times.
#ETA=/data/eta/out/now.json
#ETA=

# Line alerts for the app's "My own" (server/alerts.py):
# gtfsrt, any GTFS-Realtime ServiceAlerts feed (the Ministry of Transport's, whose address it sends on request to
# ptsupport@mot.gov.il), asked every five minutes and never more than 12 times an hour, with the headers it wants as a
# JSON object; off (the default), none. The phone downloads the whole list and finds its lines in it itself.
#ALERTS_SOURCE=gtfsrt
#ALERTS_URL=https://example.com/service-alerts.pb
#ALERTS_HEADERS={"Authorization": "Bearer ..."}
# The alerts in English too, translated once each by kav-translate (the translate profile above), or any server that
# answers LibreTranslate's /translate.
#TRANSLATE_URL=http://translate:5000
# Its API key, for a translator elsewhere that wants one.
#TRANSLATE_KEY=

# A timetable and map of your own for the planner (any GTFS zip, any OpenStreetMap .osm.pbf), instead of the ministry's
# timetable and OpenStreetMap's Israel:
#GTFS_URL=https://example.com/my-gtfs.zip
#OSM_URL=https://example.com/my-area.osm.pbf

# A step of the nightly build replaced by a command of your own, or "skip" (server/selfhost/README.md, "Build steps").
# Your scripts go in ./stages, seen as /stages in the build container.
#STAGE_PREPARE=
#STAGE_AREA=
#STAGE_WALKS=
#STAGE_PREDICT=python3 /stages/my_predict.py
#STAGE_CLOCKS=
#STAGE_GRAPH=
#STAGE_BUNDLE=

# Everything below works as it is.

# Where kav-live listens for that proxy: 127.0.0.1 keeps it to this machine, 0.0.0.0 opens it to your network.
#LISTEN=127.0.0.1
#PORT=8080

# When the nightly build runs, Israel time.
#REBUILD_AT=00:30

# Memory for the nightly planner build and for each planner while it answers, in GB: all of Israel 15 and 5, one area
# 4 and 2. The setup page sets them for one area and leaves them out (these defaults) otherwise.
#BUILD_MEMORY=15
#PLANNER_MEMORY=5

# The setup page: where it listens. It answers only this machine's own network either way.
#SETUP_LISTEN=0.0.0.0
#SETUP_PORT=8090

# The ministry's addresses, the second tried when the first fails.
#MOT_URL=https://moran.mot.gov.il/Channels/HTTPChannel/SmQuery/2.8/json
#MOT_URL_BACKUP=https://moran-t.mot.gov.il/Channels/HTTPChannel/SmQuery/2.8/json
